\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

We repurpose four detection scores as depth predictors using the same XGBoost framework and LOGO splits (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.png}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) for all methods under 8-gen pooled evaluation. Our 7-feature ensemble (diamond) matches multi-detector ensembles (squares) within 0.3\,pp at less than half the inference cost. Right panel: inference latency (ms/text) and GPU memory.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Feat.} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{ms/text} \\
\midrule
4-det.\ + surface     & 6 & \textbf{0.795} & \textbf{0.708} & 22.8 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 4 & 0.785 & 0.700 & 22.8 \\
7-feature (ours)      & 7 & 0.774 & 0.705 & \textbf{9.7} \\
Avg-LogProb           & 1 & 0.707 & 0.673 & --- \\
FastDetectGPT         & 1 & 0.660 & 0.569 & --- \\
LD-Score              & 1 & 0.630 & 0.675 & --- \\
Binoculars            & 1 & 0.411 & 0.539 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Depth classification (8-gen pooled LOGO). All methods use XGBoost with $n_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max_\text{depth}{=}6$. Binary: gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+. Inference cost: 7-feature requires one 410M reference model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles require two models totaling 1.4B parameters (2{,}868\,MB).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Our 7-feature ensemble outperforms all individual baselines by 6--11\,pp in 3-class F1; multi-detector ensembles achieve moderately higher 3-class F1 (0.785--0.795 vs.\ 0.774) with comparable binary F1 ($\leq$0.3\,pp gap) at 2.4$\times$ the computational cost.


\subsection{Downstream Utility}
\label{sec:results-downstream}

If depth fingerprinting reliably identifies recursive text, can it improve data curation?
Fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on five data variants (3 seeds each) shows that at equal sample count ($n$\,=\,3{,}951), depth-aware selection is practically equivalent to random subsampling (TOST $\pm$2\% margin, $p = 1.1 \times 10^{-4}$), but data quantity dominates: the full dataset ($n$\,=\,12{,}299) yields significantly lower perplexity (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).
Depth profiling serves a diagnostic rather than prescriptive role: the practical value lies in corpus-level auditing, not sample-level filtering.

